{#
  The ranked risks, shared by both reports. Ranked by risk score rather than
  severity, and the table says so under itself: the order is the argument.
#}
<h2 class="section-title" id="top-risks">What is worst right now</h2>
{% if report.top_risks %}
<p class="lede">
  The open risks CloudGuard ranks highest, worst first. Each is scored from the
  finding's severity and the asset it sits on: how critical it is, how
  sensitive its data, and how exposed.
</p>
<table class="data">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th class="rank">#</th>
      <th>Risk</th>
      <th class="sev-col">Level</th>
      <th class="score-col num">Risk score</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {% for risk in report.top_risks %}
    {% set score = risk.risk_score | round(0) | int %}
    <tr>
      <td class="rank">{{ loop.index }}</td>
      <td>{{ risk.title }}</td>
      <td class="sev-col"><span class="sev sev-{{ risk.risk_level }}">{{ risk.risk_level | title }}</span></td>
      <td class="score-col">
        <table class="meter">
          <tr>
            <td>
              <div class="track">
                {% if score > 0 %}
                <div class="fill" style="width: {{ [score, 100] | min }}%"></div>
                {% endif %}
              </div>
            </td>
            <td class="meter-value">{{ score }}</td>
          </tr>
        </table>
      </td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
<p class="footnote">
  A finding is what CloudGuard observed. A risk is what that observation means
  for this asset, with this data, at this level of exposure — which is why the
  order here is not the order of severity alone.
</p>
{% else %}
<p class="muted">No risks are currently ranked for this organization.</p>
{% endif %}
